"## Important caveat before the obligations\n\nA grep of the installed package (`tablegpt/utils.py` and the whole `tablegpt` tree) returns **no matches** for `safe_divide` or any `divide` definition. The only module-level functions in `utils.py` are:\n\n```\npath_from_uri, file_extension, read_df, _read_df, detect_file_encodings, filter_contents, filter_content\n```\n\nSo the obligations below are relative to a **hypothetical specification** of `safe_divide`. Nothing in the codebase currently discharges them — the \"existence\" and \"correctness\" obligations are, as it stands, unsatisfied rather than merely unproven.\n\nNotation: signature \\( \\text{safe\\_divide}: \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Q} \\); domain predicate \\( \\mathrm{Int} \\); the claim's consequent is \\( \\text{safe\\_divide}(a,b) = a/b \\).\n\n---\n\n### A. Well-definedness obligations\n\n1. **Totality / definedness.** \\( \\forall a \\forall b\\, \\bigl(\\mathrm{Int}(a) \\land \\mathrm{Int}(b) \\land b \\neq 0 \\rightarrow \\exists! r\\, \\bigl(r = \\text{safe\\_divide}(a,b)\\bigr)\\bigr) \\) — the function yields exactly one value whenever the precondition holds.\n\n2. **Codomain typing.** \\( \\forall a \\forall b\\, \\bigl(\\mathrm{Int}(a) \\land \\mathrm{Int}(b) \\land b \\neq 0 \\rightarrow \\text{safe\\_divide}(a,b) \\in \\mathbb{Q}\\bigr) \\) — note the codomain is \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\), **not** \\( \\mathbb{Z} \\); this is the obligation most often silently dropped when the claim is written for integers.\n\n3. **Precondition satisfiability.** \\( \\exists a \\exists b\\, \\bigl(\\mathrm{Int}(a) \\land \\mathrm{Int}(b) \\land b \\neq 0\\bigr) \\) — non-vacuity; otherwise every subsequent obligation is trivially true.\n\n4. **Denominator guard.** For every call site, the actual arguments must be discharged against \\( b \\neq 0 \\). Since no call sites exist, this is currently **vacuous**.\n\n---\n\n### B. Functional-correctness obligations\n\n5. **Main postcondition (the claim itself).** \\( \\forall a \\forall b\\, \\bigl(\\mathrm{Int}(a) \\land \\mathrm{Int}(b) \\land b \\neq 0 \\rightarrow \\text{safe\\_divide}(a,b) = a/b\\bigr) \\).\n\n6. **Zero-numerator case.** \\( \\forall b\\, \\bigl(\\mathrm{Int}(b) \\land b \\neq 0 \\rightarrow \\text{safe\\_divide}(0,b) = 0\\bigr) \\).\n\n7. **Rational-preservation case.** \\( \\forall a \\forall b\\, \\bigl(\\mathrm{Int}(a) \\land \\mathrm{Int}(b) \\land b \\neq 0 \\land b \\nmid a \\rightarrow \\text{safe\\_divide}(a,b) \\notin \\mathbb{Z}\\bigr) \\), consistent with obligation 2 — this is what rules out an int-truncating implementation, e.g. `a // b` or `int(a / b)`.\n\n8. **Sign obligation.** \\( \\forall a \\forall b\\, \\bigl(\\mathrm{Int}(a) \\land \\mathrm{Int}(b) \\land b \\neq 0 \\rightarrow \\text{sgn}\\,\\text{safe\\_divide}(a,b) = \\text{sgn}(a)\\cdot\\text{sgn}(b)\\bigr) \\).\n\n---\n\n### C. Behaviour outside the specified domain\n\n9. **Undefined/zero-denominator behaviour.** For \\( b = 0 \\), the claim imposes no postcondition. A total-function implementation must fix a convention: either raise (partial function, \\( \\nexists r\\; R(a,0,r) \\)), or return a designated \\( \\bot \\). This **must be stated explicitly**, or obligations 1–2 are ambiguous.\n\n---\n\n### D. Consistency and invariant obligations\n\n10. **Self-consistency.** \\( \\forall a \\forall b\\, \\bigl(b \\neq 0 \\rightarrow \\text{safe\\_divide}(a,b) = \\text{safe\\_divide}(a,b)\\bigr) \\) and the deterministic-single-valuedness already required by obligation 1 — no two distinct results for the same input.\n\n11. **Invariance under the same divisor/numerator scaling.** \\( \\forall a \\forall b \\forall k\\, \\bigl(\\mathrm{Int}(k) \\land k \\neq 0 \\land b \\neq 0 \\rightarrow \\text{safe\\_divide}(a,b) = \\text{safe\\_divide}(ka, kb)\\bigr) \\).\n\n12. **Reciprocal invariant.** \\( \\forall a \\forall b\\, \\bigl(a \\neq 0 \\land b \\neq 0 \\rightarrow \\text{safe\\_divide}(a,b) = 1/\\text{safe\\_divide}(b,a)\\bigr) \\).\n\n13. **Composition/round-trip invariant.** \\( \\forall a \\forall b\\, \\bigl(b \\neq 0 \\rightarrow \\text{safe\\_divide}(a,b) \\cdot b = a\\bigr) \\) — this is the strongest practical check tying the function to ordinary division.\n\n---\n\n### E. Discharge status (as of this analysis)\n\n| Obligation | Status |\n|---|---|\n| 1, 2 (definedness) | **Unsatisfied** — no such function is defined anywhere in `tablegpt` |\n| 3 (satisfiability) | Trivially satisfied |\n| 4 (call-site guards) | Vacuously satisfied — no call sites |\n| 5–8, 11–13 (correctness/invariants) | **Unsatisfied / not applicable** — no implementation to verify |\n| 9 (zero-denominator) | **Unspecified** — no convention present in the code |\n\n**Bottom line:** the claim is stated over a symbol with no definition in the codebase, so it cannot be proven or refuted from the current source. Obligations 1, 2, and 5–8 collectively pin down what `safe_divide` *must* be for the claim to hold: a total, \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\)-valued, exact-rational division guarded by \\( b \\neq 0 \\). Any candidate implementation using `//`, `int(a/b)`, or unguarded `/` would fail obligations 2, 7, or 9 respectively."